  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are presented without specifying the case study city, sample size, or geographic boundaries used to validate the modified gravity model.",
      "what": "This study develops a modified gravity model that incorporates multiple travel impedances, such as transit transfers, to measure dynamic employment accessibility across morning peak, off-peak, and evening peak hours. The model was applied to compare accessibility levels between urban villages and commercial housing, revealing that commercial housing consistently maintains higher accessibility and that urban village residents face more severe vertical inequity in reaching jobs.",
      "why": "By accounting for the complex, multi-transfer transit journeys that low-income urban village residents actually rely on, the study quantifies a previously obscured gap in employment access and demonstrates that spatial inequities are compounded by housing type."
    }
  },
